PART 6: WITHIN-FIBER FRACTION — ANALYTICAL APPROACH
------------------------------------------------------------

  The within-fiber fraction = P(a random flip preserves scores).

  For a uniformly random tournament T on n vertices:
  - Pick a random arc (i,j) with i→j.
  - score[i] = s_i, score[j] = s_j.
  - The flip preserves the sorted score iff |s_i - s_j| ≤ 1.

  So the fraction = E[# arcs connecting vertices with |score diff| ≤ 1] / C(n,2).

  At n=3: every tournament has all scores within ±1 of each other
  (scores are either 0,1,2 or 1,1,1). Fraction = 1/2.

  The fraction EQUALS:
    (1/2^C(n,2)) × Σ_T Σ_{(i,j)} 1[|s_i(T) - s_j(T)| ≤ 1] / C(n,2)
  where the sum is over all labeled tournaments T.

  n=3: P(|score_i - score_j| ≤ 1) = 3/4 = 0.750000
  n=4: P(|score_i - score_j| ≤ 1) = 11/16 = 0.687500
  n=5: P(|score_i - score_j| ≤ 1) = 41/64 = 0.640625

  NOTE: This is the probability that a random pair of vertices
  in a random tournament has adjacent scores.
  The within-fiber fraction is DIFFERENT (it also requires
  the arc to go from the higher-scored to the lower-scored vertex).


PART 7: THE SLOPE b(n) = (1 - H_max(n)) / S₂_max(n)
